WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.458
When Curiosity radios home from Mars, the signal travels in a straight line at the speed of light.

00:00:06.458 --> 00:00:11.664
And it still takes anywhere from three minutes to twenty-two minutes to arrive.

00:00:11.664 --> 00:00:17.200
Three minutes when Mars and Earth are on the same side of the Sun, closest together.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.658
Twenty-two minutes when Mars is on the far side of the Sun, on the other side of the inner system.

00:00:23.658 --> 00:00:25.042
The radio isn't slow.

00:00:25.042 --> 00:00:26.492
The universe is large.

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:31.039
This is why no human on Earth has ever driven Curiosity in real time.

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:39.012
A joystick command — "turn left" — would arrive on Mars somewhere between three and twenty-two minutes after you sent it.

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:44.746
By then the rover would already be eight meters past the rock you were trying to avoid.

00:00:44.746 --> 00:00:47.052
So Curiosity doesn't have a driver.

00:00:47.052 --> 00:00:48.172
It has a planner.

00:00:48.172 --> 00:00:55.290
Each Martian day — each sol — the team at JPL sends a sequence of commands the rover executes autonomously .

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.135
Drive forward twelve meters.

00:00:57.135 --> 00:00:57.464
Stop.

00:00:57.464 --> 00:00:58.650
Take seven photos.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.286
Choose the safest of three onward paths.

00:01:01.286 --> 00:01:02.407
The rover sleeps.

00:01:02.407 --> 00:01:03.263
Earth sleeps.

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:04.120
Mars rotates.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:16.246
And when both planets wake the next sol, fourteen minutes apart, the next set of commands flies through the dark to the rover that's already been working in the dark for sixteen hours.

00:01:16.246 --> 00:01:17.366
Fourteen minutes.

00:01:17.366 --> 00:01:17.959
Not slow.

00:01:17.959 --> 00:01:18.552
Just far.

